# 79. Features NOT to Build Too Early

Do not start with:

- full accounting;
- payroll;
- tax filing;
- ERP inventory;
- full project-management suite;
- complex custom workflow builder;
- call-center infrastructure;
- native iOS/Android apps;
- dozens of integrations.

First make the core operational loop excellent.

---

# 80. Main User Journey — Solo Business

```text
Register
→ Create workspace
→ Add services
→ Set working hours
→ Share booking link
→ Client books
→ Appointment appears
→ Reminder sent
→ Service completed
→ Payment recorded
→ Follow-up
→ AI summarizes day
```

---

# 81. Main User Journey — Team Business

```text
New request
→ AI / admin qualifies request
→ Assign staff/resource
→ Schedule
→ Client confirmation
→ Staff completes work
→ Invoice
→ Payment
→ Follow-up
→ Analytics
```

---

# 82. Main User Journey — Private Appointment

Owner creates appointment:

```text
Client: Example Client
Service: Consultation
Time: 15:00
Visibility: OWNER_ONLY
Financial bucket: PRIVATE
```

Other employee calendar:

```text
15:00 — Busy
```

Owner calendar:

```text
15:00
Example Client
Consultation
€150
Private
```

Finance owner view:

```text
Main revenue:    €8,450
Private revenue: €1,320
Combined:        €9,770
```

This workflow is mandatory.

---

# 83. Main User Journey — AI Secretary

Owner:

> “Find Frau Müller and move her Friday appointment to the first free slot after 15:00.”

System:

1. identifies client;
2. identifies appointment;
3. checks permissions;
4. checks availability;
5. returns proposed move;
6. owner confirms;
7. system updates appointment;
8. audit log written;
9. optional client notification sent.

---

# 84. Design Deliverables Before Heavy Development

Before implementing the entire app, create these polished screens:

1. Login
2. Onboarding
3. Dashboard
4. Calendar
5. Appointment drawer/modal
6. Client list
7. Client profile
8. Assistant
9. Finance
10. Settings
11. Public booking
12. Mobile Today screen

These screens establish the full visual system.

---

# 85. Component System

Build reusable components:

```text
AppShell
Sidebar
MobileNav
TopBar

PageHeader
SectionHeader

Card
MetricCard
ActionCard
AttentionCard

Button
IconButton
Dropdown
Tabs
Chip
Badge

Input
Textarea
Select
Combobox
DatePicker
TimePicker
MoneyInput

Drawer
Modal
Popover

DataTable
EmptyState

Calendar
AppointmentCard
TimeGrid

ClientAvatar
ClientSummary

AssistantMessage
AssistantActionCard
AssistantComposer

FinancialBucketBadge
VisibilityBadge
```

Avoid page-specific duplicated UI.

---

# 86. Status Visual Language

Use calm contextual status tokens.

Example:

```text
Confirmed    green
Pending      yellow
Cancelled    muted/red
No-show      red
Completed    neutral/dark
Private      violet/neutral special marker
Owner-only   lock icon + neutral
```

Do not rely on color alone.

---

# 87. Responsive Breakpoints

Design intentionally for:

- mobile;
- tablet;
- laptop;
- desktop.

Mobile screens should prioritize action.

Example mobile home:

```text
Good morning

[ + New ]

Today
09:00 Müller
10:30 Schmidt
12:00 Busy
...

Needs attention
2 confirmations
1 unpaid invoice
```

---

# 88. Performance

Goals:

- fast first load;
- route-level code splitting;
- server rendering where useful;
- optimistic UI for safe actions;
- virtualize large lists/calendar where needed;
- avoid giant client bundles;
- cache carefully without leaking tenant data.

---

# 89. Testing

Minimum:

- unit tests for business rules;
- integration tests for permissions;
- booking conflict tests;
- financial bucket tests;
- private appointment visibility tests;
- AI tool authorization tests;
- E2E for critical workflows.

Critical test:

> A user without `private_records.view` must never receive private appointment details from API, UI, search or AI.

---

# 90. Development Rules for Claude / Coding Agent

## 90.1 Do not build the entire product in one pass

Work in phases.

For each task:

1. inspect relevant files only;
2. explain intended change briefly;
3. implement;
4. typecheck;
5. lint;
6. test relevant functionality;
7. report changed files.

## 90.2 Preserve architecture

Do not:

- duplicate data models;
- hard-code workspace IDs;
- bypass RLS;
- bypass permission checks;
- put secrets in client code;
- let AI call raw database queries directly.

## 90.3 Preserve design

Do not introduce:

- random gradients;
- generic dashboard templates;
- inconsistent radii;
- arbitrary shadows;
- new colors without tokens;
- different typography per page;
- Tailwind CSS;
- utility-class-heavy styling;
- inline style sprawl.

Use the design system.

### Styling rule — mandatory

This project must NOT use Tailwind CSS.

Preferred styling approach:

```text
CSS Modules / SCSS Modules
+
CSS Custom Properties
+
Design Tokens
+
shared component-level styles
```

Global design tokens should define:

```text
colors
spacing
radii
typography
shadows
z-index
motion
breakpoints
```

Example:

```css
:root {
  --color-ink: #111111;
  --color-paper: #f6f5f2;
  --color-surface: #ffffff;
  --color-muted: #6e6e6e;

  --radius-sm: 12px;
  --radius-md: 20px;
  --radius-lg: 28px;

  --space-1: 4px;
  --space-2: 8px;
  --space-3: 12px;
  --space-4: 16px;
  --space-6: 24px;
  --space-8: 32px;
}
```

Component styles should live close to components, for example:

```text
components/
  Button/
    Button.tsx
    Button.module.css
```

The goal is a controlled, original visual system inspired by Diia's clarity, not a generic utility-framework look.

